 !!! Note
     The minimum last stake amount can be configured using `last_stake_amount_min_ratio` - which defaults to 0.5 (50%). This means that the minimum stake amount that's ever used is `stake_amount * 0.5`. This avoids very low stake amounts, that are close to the minimum tradable amount for the pair and can be refused by the exchange.
 
+#### Limiting currency exposure
+
+If you trade several pairs that are all backed by, or priced against, the same underlying currency (e.g. several `*/USDT:USDT` futures, or several spot pairs sharing the same base currency), a single adverse move in that currency can hit multiple open trades at once. `max_currency_exposure` limits how much of your total tradable capital may be committed at the same time to trades sharing the same underlying (base) currency, across all open trades - regardless of how attractive any individual new trade looks in isolation.
+
+The "underlying currency" of a pair is its base currency - for example both `BTC/USDT` and `BTC/USDT:USDT` count towards the same `BTC` exposure bucket.
+
+For example, with `max_currency_exposure=0.3` (30%) and a tradable capital of 1000 USDT, no more than 300 USDT may be committed across all simultaneously open BTC trades. If you already have 250 USDT tied up in open BTC trades and a new BTC signal would normally use 100 USDT, the stake is reduced to 50 USDT to stay under the limit; if even the minimum tradable stake for the pair doesn't fit under the remaining limit, the trade is not opened.
+
+This setting applies identically whether the bot is trading live/dry-run or running a backtest, and any resizing/skip decision is logged the same way other stake-sizing adjustments are (see [Static stake amount](#static-stake-amount)).
+
+!!! Note
+    This setting is disabled (no cap) by default.
+
 #### Static stake amount
 
 The `stake_amount` configuration statically configures the amount of stake-currency your bot will use for each trade.
